::ENG4_U04_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Sources on flexible schedules show fewer interruptions, possible slower communication, and better results with shared calendars. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Flexible schedules solve every workplace problem.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence and ignores the communication concern.
=B. Flexible schedules can support focused work when teams establish clear communication routines.#Correct. This connects all source ideas without overstating them.
~C. Communication never matters when employees work flexibly.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source set.
~D. Shared calendars are unrelated to flexible scheduling.#Incorrect. The source set presents shared calendars as a condition for success.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: A counselor report found application completion increased during structured work sessions with school resources. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. The report says counselors should complete applications for students.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning of the source.
~B. The report proves every student finished every application.#Incorrect. This exaggerates the evidence.
~C. The report is mainly about library furniture.#Incorrect. This is not the source focus.
=D. The report found more applications were completed when students had structured time and school resources.#Correct. This accurately restates the source in new wording.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Source set: Later library hours may help seniors with applications, but require supervision. Which claim is strongest?
{
=A. Schools should consider later library hours during application season if supervision and safety procedures are included.#Correct. This claim uses the benefit and the condition.
~B. Libraries are nice places to study.#Incorrect. This is not a precise argument.
~C. Schools should keep libraries open all night every night.#Incorrect. This goes beyond the evidence.
~D. Seniors do not need application support.#Incorrect. This ignores the evidence.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence best uses clear conventions to connect sources?
{
~A. The sources about applications and library hours, students need help, because jobs.#Incorrect. This sentence is unclear and incomplete.
~B. Students have jobs libraries are open applications happen.#Incorrect. This run-on does not show relationships.
=C. Because some seniors lack quiet work time, later library hours could support application completion if supervision is planned.#Correct. This sentence clearly connects cause, condition, and claim.
~D. Later library hours help because help is helpful.#Incorrect. This repeats wording without clear reasoning.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Sources on shaded seating show heat-stress benefits, maintenance concerns, and longer downtown visits. What relationship do the sources show?
{
~A. Shaded seating has no possible benefits.#Incorrect. This ignores two source benefits.
~B. Maintenance concerns prove seating should never be built.#Incorrect. This treats a condition as a rejection.
=C. A public-space improvement may have health and economic benefits but needs maintenance planning.#Correct. This describes the full source relationship.
~D. Downtown visits are unrelated to public seating.#Incorrect. One source directly connects seating and longer visits.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: A pilot program reported lower plastic waste when water stations were placed near entrances and food areas. Which paraphrase is best?
{
=A. The pilot reported less plastic waste when water stations were located where event visitors could easily reach them.#Correct. This accurately restates the source and preserves the placement idea.
~B. The pilot program banned all food areas and entrances.#Incorrect. This changes the source meaning.
~C. The pilot proved water stations work in every location.#Incorrect. This overgeneralizes.
~D. The pilot focused mainly on ticket prices.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to the source.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim best grows from sources showing AI tools can support practice but weaken independent reasoning when overused?
{
~A. AI tools should write all student responses.#Incorrect. This ignores the concern about independent reasoning.
~B. AI tools have no educational use.#Incorrect. This ignores the practice benefit.
~C. AI tools are only about spelling.#Incorrect. This is too narrow and unsupported.
=D. Schools should teach students to use AI tools for practice and revision while requiring students to explain their own reasoning.#Correct. This claim balances benefit and concern.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which transition best signals a tension between source benefits and concerns?
{
~A. Also#Incorrect. This adds information but does not show tension.
=B. However#Correct. This transition signals contrast or complication between source ideas.
~C. For example#Incorrect. This introduces an example, not a contrast.
~D. Therefore#Incorrect. This signals result, not tension.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Source A supports a policy, Source B identifies a practical challenge, and Source C shows a successful pilot. What should synthesis do?
{
~A. Ignore Source B because challenges weaken an argument.#Incorrect. Challenges can help create a more credible argument.
~B. Copy all three sources word for word.#Incorrect. Synthesis requires accurate restatement and connection, not copying.
=C. Explain how the benefit, challenge, and pilot evidence work together.#Correct. Synthesis connects source roles into a larger understanding.
~D. Choose only the easiest source to use.#Incorrect. Synthesis uses multiple sources.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Which paraphrase avoids changing meaning?
{
=A. Original: Students reported more review sessions. Paraphrase: Students said they reviewed more often.#Correct. This preserves the meaning in new wording.
~B. Original: Students reported more review sessions. Paraphrase: Students never reviewed before.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~C. Original: Students reported more review sessions. Paraphrase: Teachers forced every student to review.#Incorrect. This adds unsupported information.
~D. Original: Students reported more review sessions. Paraphrase: Review sessions were banned.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which synthesis would best prepare for an argument claim?
{
~A. One source exists and then another source exists.#Incorrect. This does not explain a relationship.
~B. The sources are about many things.#Incorrect. This is too vague to support a claim.
~C. The sources are long and probably important.#Incorrect. This evaluates length rather than meaning.
=D. The sources show that the policy has measurable benefits, but it needs clear implementation rules.#Correct. This creates a usable basis for an argument claim.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is most precise and readable?
{
~A. The policy, which is a thing, is good, like, because sources.#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
=B. The sources show that the policy can work when planners address cost, staffing, and access.#Correct. This uses clear structure and precise nouns.
~C. Source things show stuff and problems.#Incorrect. This lacks precision.
~D. Since the sources have data and concerns, there is evidence.#Incorrect. This is vague and incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which action comes before writing a synthesis statement?
{
~A. Choosing a dramatic opinion first.#Incorrect. Synthesis should grow from evidence, not begin with unsupported opinion.
=B. Understanding and paraphrasing each source accurately.#Correct. Accurate understanding must come before source connection.
~C. Removing all sources that disagree.#Incorrect. Differences among sources can strengthen synthesis.
~D. Writing a conclusion without reading.#Incorrect. This skips the research process.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Why is paraphrase important before synthesis?
{
~A. It lets the writer change a source to match an opinion.#Incorrect. Paraphrase must preserve meaning.
~B. It removes the need for evidence.#Incorrect. Paraphrase is a way to use evidence, not avoid it.
=C. It confirms the writer understands each source before connecting sources.#Correct. Accurate paraphrase supports accurate synthesis.
~D. It makes all sources say the same thing.#Incorrect. Paraphrase should not erase differences.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim is too broad for evidence about one city pilot program?
{
=A. Every city in the world must build water stations immediately.#Correct. This is too broad for one pilot program.
~B. The pilot suggests placement affects station use.#Incorrect. This stays within the evidence.
~C. The city should study station placement before expansion.#Incorrect. This is a reasonable claim from the evidence.
~D. Placement near entrances may improve access.#Incorrect. This matches the evidence.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which revision improves clarity?
{
~A. The sources good and not good and maybe city.#Incorrect. This is not clear academic writing.
~B. Sources, which are there, discuss things in different ways.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. City seating improves and maintenance.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
=D. The sources suggest that shaded seating can improve public comfort if the city also funds maintenance.#Correct. This revision clearly connects benefit and condition.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] What is a tension in research synthesis?
{
~A. A spelling mistake in a source title.#Incorrect. That is an editing issue, not a synthesis tension.
~B. A source that is easy to read.#Incorrect. Readability is not the same as tension.
=C. A meaningful difference or complication between sources.#Correct. Tension helps writers develop balanced analysis.
~D. A sentence copied exactly from a source.#Incorrect. Copying is not a source relationship.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Which sentence changes the source meaning?
{
=A. Source: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: Response times always stopped completely.#Correct. This changes sometimes into always and exaggerates.
~B. Source: Some students lacked quiet work time. Paraphrase: Some students did not have quiet time for school tasks.#Incorrect. This preserves meaning.
~C. Source: The project required maintenance. Paraphrase: The project needed upkeep.#Incorrect. This preserves meaning.
~D. Source: Shared calendars helped teams. Paraphrase: Teams benefited from using shared calendars.#Incorrect. This preserves meaning.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which evidence best supports a claim that structured school work sessions help seniors complete applications?
{
~A. A report about cafeteria menus.#Incorrect. This does not relate to applications.
=B. A counselor report showing increased application completion during structured work sessions.#Correct. This directly supports the claim.
~C. A quote about library paint color.#Incorrect. This is not relevant to application completion.
~D. A source about elementary recess.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to seniors and applications.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a semicolon?
{
~A. Structured sessions help students; because applications are hard.#Incorrect. The second part is not an independent clause.
~B. Structured sessions; help students complete applications.#Incorrect. The semicolon separates a subject from its verb.
~C. Structured sessions help; students complete applications.#Incorrect. This incorrectly splits related sentence parts.
=D. Structured sessions provide time and resources; they can help seniors complete applications.#Correct. Both sides are complete related clauses.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which phrase best signals synthesis?
{
=A. Taken together, the sources suggest...#Correct. This phrase signals that multiple sources are being connected.
~B. I did not read the second source...#Incorrect. This does not show synthesis.
~C. This source is longer than the others...#Incorrect. Length is not source relationship.
~D. My opinion has no evidence...#Incorrect. This is not research synthesis.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Which paraphrase is most accurate for "The project was most effective when teams used shared calendars"?
{
~A. The project failed because calendars are confusing.#Incorrect. This reverses the source meaning.
~B. The project did not involve teams.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
=C. The project worked best when teams coordinated through shared calendars.#Correct. This preserves the meaning in new wording.
~D. The project was only about paper calendars.#Incorrect. This adds unsupported detail.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which option best explains how synthesis supports argument writing?
{
~A. It lets writers avoid making a claim.#Incorrect. Synthesis often prepares the claim.
~B. It replaces reasoning with longer quotations.#Incorrect. Synthesis supports reasoning; it does not replace it.
~C. It removes all source differences.#Incorrect. Synthesis often explains differences.
=D. It helps writers form claims that reflect patterns and complications in the evidence.#Correct. This is why synthesis matters in argument writing.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence avoids a comma splice?
{
~A. The sources show benefits, they also show challenges.#Incorrect. This joins two independent clauses with only a comma.
=B. The sources show benefits, but they also identify practical challenges.#Correct. The coordinating conjunction properly joins the clauses.
~C. The sources show benefits, and challenges are also discussed, because.#Incorrect. This is unclear and incomplete.
~D. Benefits appear in the sources, challenges.#Incorrect. This is fragmented.
}

::ENG4_U04_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which source relationship would most likely lead to a qualified claim?
{
=A. Two sources show benefits, while one source identifies a condition for success.#Correct. This relationship supports a claim with limits or conditions.
~B. All sources are unrelated to the research question.#Incorrect. Unrelated sources cannot support a strong claim.
~C. One source is copied without understanding.#Incorrect. Copying is not synthesis.
~D. The writer ignores the source that complicates the issue.#Incorrect. Ignoring complications weakens the claim.
}
